## Original Content
<p>As federal borrowing rules change, graduate education has a chance to move beyond sticker price and focus on flexibility, transparency, and long term value. For students, the question is not only how to pay for a degree, but whether the degree is designed to be worth the investment.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://blog.coursera.org/as-u-s-graduate-financing-changes-affordability-by-design-matters-more/">As U.S. graduate financing changes, affordability by design matters more</a> appeared first on <a href="https://blog.coursera.org">Coursera Blog</a>.</p>

## Summary
As federal borrowing rules for graduate education change in the U.S., institutions are shifting focus to affordability by design, emphasizing flexibility, transparency, and long-term value over sticker price. Students must evaluate whether their degree is designed to be worth the investment.

## Key Points
- Federal borrowing rules for graduate education in the U.S. are undergoing changes.
- Affordability by design is becoming more critical, moving beyond just the sticker price.
- Key elements include flexibility, transparency, and long-term value in degree programs.
- Students should assess the overall worth of their degree, not just the cost.


